1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,374
This is a messy downloads folder.

2
00:00:02,374 --> 00:00:05,015
Fifteen random items, all mixed together.

3
00:00:05,015 --> 00:00:05,658
Now watch.

4
00:00:05,658 --> 00:00:12,162
One command, and everything lands in Images, Documents, Audio, Video and Archives, in under a second.

5
00:00:12,862 --> 00:00:16,686
In the next few minutes, you'll build this exact script from scratch.

6
00:00:16,686 --> 00:00:21,618
About thirty lines of Python, nothing to install, and it works on Windows, Mac and Linux.

7
00:00:21,618 --> 00:00:27,603
And stay for the second half, because this kind of script can silently destroy a file when you run it twice.

8
00:00:27,603 --> 00:00:30,983
I'll show you exactly how, and the one line that prevents it.

9
00:00:31,683 --> 00:00:32,589
Here's the plan.

10
00:00:32,589 --> 00:00:34,684
First, list everything in the folder.

11
00:00:34,684 --> 00:00:38,252
Second, decide where each file belongs, based on its extension.

12
00:00:38,252 --> 00:00:42,386
Third, a dry run, which prints what would happen without moving anything.

13
00:00:42,386 --> 00:00:44,198
Fourth, move the files for real.

14
00:00:44,198 --> 00:00:46,860
And fifth, make it safe to run again and again.

15
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:50,113
Don't start on your real downloads folder.

16
00:00:50,113 --> 00:00:55,462
Make a new folder on your desktop called sorter, and copy your downloads folder into it.

17
00:00:55,462 --> 00:00:57,893
That way, any mistake happens on a copy.

18
00:00:57,893 --> 00:01:03,242
Open a terminal inside sorter, like we did in episode one, and list what's in Downloads.

19
00:01:03,242 --> 00:01:05,552
On Windows, type d i r instead of l s.

20
00:01:06,252 --> 00:01:10,817
Now create a file called sort downloads dot p y, next to the Downloads folder.

21
00:01:10,817 --> 00:01:13,861
Import Path from pathlib, and point it at Downloads.

22
00:01:13,861 --> 00:01:19,070
Then loop over iterdir, which hands you everything inside the folder, one item at a time.

23
00:01:19,070 --> 00:01:22,406
We wrap it in sorted, so the order is the same every run.

24
00:01:22,406 --> 00:01:25,098
For now, just print each name, and its suffix.

25
00:01:25,798 --> 00:01:28,556
Run it with python sort downloads dot p y.

26
00:01:28,556 --> 00:01:31,315
Every file shows up next to its extension.

27
00:01:31,315 --> 00:01:33,351
But look at project dash files.

28
00:01:33,351 --> 00:01:36,568
It has no suffix at all, because it's not a file.

29
00:01:36,568 --> 00:01:37,488
It's a folder.

30
00:01:37,488 --> 00:01:40,378
Remember that, because it's about to matter.

31
00:01:41,078 --> 00:01:42,592
A quick note on suffix.

32
00:01:42,592 --> 00:01:46,740
It includes the dot, and it keeps the original capital letters.

33
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:53,128
Cameras love capital J P G, so we'll always convert the suffix to lowercase before we compare it.

34
00:01:53,128 --> 00:01:55,828
That one detail saves a lot of confusion.

35
00:01:56,528 --> 00:01:57,522
Now the rules.

36
00:01:57,522 --> 00:02:00,433
Images get J P G, J P E G, P N G and GIF.

37
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:03,913
Documents get P D F, Word, text, Excel and C S V.

38
00:02:03,913 --> 00:02:05,759
Audio gets M P 3 and wave.

39
00:02:05,759 --> 00:02:07,677
Video gets M P 4 and M O V.

40
00:02:07,677 --> 00:02:09,452
Archives get zip and rar.

41
00:02:09,452 --> 00:02:16,270
And anything we don't recognize goes into a folder called Other, so nothing is ever left behind.

42
00:02:16,969 --> 00:02:19,605
In Python, those rules become a dictionary.

43
00:02:19,605 --> 00:02:24,876
Each key is a folder name, and each value is a set of extensions, inside curly braces.

44
00:02:24,876 --> 00:02:31,496
A set is perfect here, because checking whether something is in a set is instant, no matter how big it gets.

45
00:02:31,496 --> 00:02:34,499
Want a new category, like Ebooks, with dot e pub?

46
00:02:34,499 --> 00:02:35,602
Just add one line.

47
00:02:36,302 --> 00:02:38,683
Next, a small function called folder for.

48
00:02:38,683 --> 00:02:44,724
It takes an extension, walks through the dictionary, and returns the first folder whose set contains it.

49
00:02:44,724 --> 00:02:47,918
If the loop finishes without a match, it returns Other.

50
00:02:47,918 --> 00:02:52,332
Keeping this in its own function makes the main loop short and easy to read.

51
00:02:53,032 --> 00:02:54,537
Remember project dash files?

52
00:02:54,537 --> 00:02:57,763
Here's the mistake almost everyone makes on their first try.

53
00:02:57,763 --> 00:03:01,741
If you don't check that each item is a file, the script moves folders too.

54
00:03:01,741 --> 00:03:04,644
On the first run, project files disappears into Other.

55
00:03:04,644 --> 00:03:06,633
And on the second run, it gets worse.

56
00:03:06,633 --> 00:03:09,375
Here's what happened when I ran that version twice.

57
00:03:09,375 --> 00:03:12,869
Archives, Audio, Documents and Images all get moved inside Other.

58
00:03:12,869 --> 00:03:15,718
Then it tries to move Other into itself, and crashes.

59
00:03:16,418 --> 00:03:18,037
The fix is a single line.

60
00:03:18,037 --> 00:03:19,331
if file dot is file.

61
00:03:19,331 --> 00:03:26,517
Only real files get touched, folders stay exactly where they are, and the script is safe to run again tomorrow.

62
00:03:27,217 --> 00:03:28,405
Here's the main loop.

63
00:03:28,405 --> 00:03:31,347
For each item that's a file, we work out the target.

64
00:03:31,347 --> 00:03:35,533
downloads, slash, folder for, gives us a path like Downloads slash Images.

65
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:39,775
Yes, in pathlib, the slash operator joins paths, on every operating system.

66
00:03:39,775 --> 00:03:41,925
Then we print where the file would go.

67
00:03:41,925 --> 00:03:44,640
And notice the last two lines are commented out.

68
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:45,658
This is a dry run.

69
00:03:46,358 --> 00:03:46,766
Run it.

70
00:03:46,766 --> 00:03:52,302
Fourteen files, fourteen decisions, and project files isn't on the list, because it's a folder.

71
00:03:52,302 --> 00:03:53,526
Read the list slowly.

72
00:03:53,526 --> 00:03:58,246
If something is heading to the wrong place, fix the dictionary, and run it again.

73
00:03:58,246 --> 00:04:00,809
Nothing has moved yet, so there's zero risk.

74
00:04:01,509 --> 00:04:02,671
Happy with the plan?

75
00:04:02,671 --> 00:04:05,457
Remove the hash symbols from the last two lines.

76
00:04:05,457 --> 00:04:11,205
target dot mkdir creates the folder, and exist ok equals true means no error if it's already there.

77
00:04:11,205 --> 00:04:13,759
Then file dot rename moves the file into it.

78
00:04:13,759 --> 00:04:17,649
In pathlib, renaming a file into another folder is how you move it.

79
00:04:17,649 --> 00:04:18,229
One catch.

80
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:22,468
If you ever move files to a different drive, use shutil dot move instead.

81
00:04:23,167 --> 00:04:23,958
Run it again.

82
00:04:23,958 --> 00:04:26,331
The same list, but this time it's real.

83
00:04:26,331 --> 00:04:33,693
List the Downloads folder, and instead of fifteen loose items, there are six tidy folders, plus project files, untouched.

84
00:04:34,393 --> 00:04:35,912
Here's the full picture.

85
00:04:35,912 --> 00:04:42,743
Every image in Images, every document in Documents, and the font file we never planned for, safely in Other.

86
00:04:43,443 --> 00:04:45,784
Now, the danger I promised at the start.

87
00:04:45,784 --> 00:04:50,700
Next week, you download a new version of report dot t x t, and run the script again.

88
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:51,519
Watch closely.

89
00:04:51,519 --> 00:04:54,854
Before the run, the report in Documents says version one.

90
00:04:54,854 --> 00:04:55,732
Run the script.

91
00:04:55,732 --> 00:04:57,370
And now it says version two.

92
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:58,716
The old report is gone.

93
00:04:58,716 --> 00:05:00,881
No warning, no error, no recycle bin.

94
00:05:01,581 --> 00:05:03,480
And it depends on your system.

95
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:07,278
On Mac and Linux, rename quietly replaces the existing file.

96
00:05:07,278 --> 00:05:14,556
On Windows, you get a file exists error instead, and the script stops halfway, with some files sorted and some not.

97
00:05:14,556 --> 00:05:16,075
Neither is what we want.

98
00:05:16,775 --> 00:05:19,116
The fix is a tiny function called unique.

99
00:05:19,116 --> 00:05:21,171
It starts with the destination path.

100
00:05:21,171 --> 00:05:24,882
While a file with that name already exists, it builds a new name.

101
00:05:24,882 --> 00:05:27,907
The stem, then a number in brackets, then the suffix.

102
00:05:27,907 --> 00:05:33,444
So report dot t x t becomes report, one, then report, two, and so on, until it finds a free name.

103
00:05:33,444 --> 00:05:35,899
Then we move the file to that path instead.

104
00:05:36,599 --> 00:05:38,908
Same test again, with the new version.

105
00:05:38,908 --> 00:05:43,889
This time, the new report lands as report, one, and both versions are still there.

106
00:05:43,889 --> 00:05:48,021
That's the line that makes this script safe to run every single day.

107
00:05:48,721 --> 00:05:51,661
Two more things live in a real downloads folder.

108
00:05:51,661 --> 00:05:58,152
Files that are still downloading, which Chrome and Edge name dot c r download, and Firefox names dot part.

109
00:05:58,152 --> 00:06:00,602
Move those mid download, and they break.

110
00:06:00,602 --> 00:06:06,543
And hidden system files, like dot D S underscore store on a Mac, or desktop dot i n i on Windows.

111
00:06:06,543 --> 00:06:16,158
So we add a skip set, and at the top of the loop, if it's not a file, starts with a dot, or has a skipped extension, continue jumps straight to the next one.

112
00:06:16,858 --> 00:06:17,558
Final test.

113
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:21,628
A new report, a half finished movie download, and a hidden file.

114
00:06:21,628 --> 00:06:23,473
Only the real new file moved.

115
00:06:23,473 --> 00:06:29,069
The download in progress and the hidden file were left alone, exactly as they should be.

116
00:06:29,769 --> 00:06:33,607
When you're confident, point it at your real downloads folder.

117
00:06:33,607 --> 00:06:37,011
Path dot home gives you your user folder on any system.

118
00:06:37,011 --> 00:06:43,138
So Path dot home, slash, Downloads, works on Windows, Mac and Linux without changing anything else.

119
00:06:43,138 --> 00:06:44,748
Do one more dry run first.

120
00:06:44,748 --> 00:06:45,181
Always.

121
00:06:45,881 --> 00:06:46,262
Bonus.

122
00:06:46,262 --> 00:06:49,690
Some people prefer folders by date instead of by type.

123
00:06:49,690 --> 00:06:54,642
A file's last modified time lives in file dot stat, dot s t underscore m time.

124
00:06:54,642 --> 00:07:05,879
datetime dot from timestamp turns that number into a date, and s t r f time, with percent capital Y, dash, percent m, gives you a folder name like twenty twenty six dash eleven.

125
00:07:06,579 --> 00:07:09,565
Run it, and your downloads are grouped by month.

126
00:07:09,565 --> 00:07:14,417
Year first, then month, so the folders also sort in time order, automatically.

127
00:07:15,117 --> 00:07:18,261
If something goes wrong, it's usually one of three things.

128
00:07:18,261 --> 00:07:19,020
Nothing moves?

129
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:22,326
The two lines at the bottom are probably still commented out.

130
00:07:22,326 --> 00:07:23,844
Permission error on Windows?

131
00:07:23,844 --> 00:07:27,151
A file is open in another program, so close it and run again.

132
00:07:27,151 --> 00:07:35,499
And file not found error means the terminal is open in the wrong folder, or Downloads is spelled differently, and capital letters matter on Mac and Linux.

133
00:07:36,199 --> 00:07:36,749
Pro tip.

134
00:07:36,749 --> 00:07:38,539
Make the dictionary yours.

135
00:07:38,539 --> 00:07:40,534
Download a lot of installers?

136
00:07:40,534 --> 00:07:45,145
Add a line for Installers, with dot e x e, dot m s i and dot d m g.

137
00:07:45,145 --> 00:07:45,902
Code files?

138
00:07:45,902 --> 00:07:47,553
Add dot p y and dot j s.

139
00:07:47,553 --> 00:07:50,650
The rest of the script doesn't change at all.

140
00:07:51,350 --> 00:07:52,120
Quick recap.

141
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:53,723
iterdir lists the folder.

142
00:07:53,723 --> 00:07:57,121
A dictionary of sets maps each extension to a folder.

143
00:07:57,121 --> 00:08:00,968
A dry run shows the plan, then mkdir and rename do the work.

144
00:08:00,968 --> 00:08:03,918
unique makes sure nothing is ever overwritten.

145
00:08:03,918 --> 00:08:07,316
And the skip check leaves unfinished downloads alone.

146
00:08:08,016 --> 00:08:16,527
Next on Shortcut Lab, we switch to Excel, with seven formulas that replace hours of manual work, like X lookup, filter, and unique.

147
00:08:16,527 --> 00:08:18,606
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
